= 1
{parent=2009}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

The <matrix exponential> is the absolutely convergent series $e^{tA}=\sum_{n=0}^\infty t^nA^n/n!$, with derivative $Ae^{tA}$ and inverse $e^{-tA}$. If $K^T=-K$, then $(e^{tK})^Te^{tK}=e^{-tK}e^{tK}=I$. Its <determinant> is <continuous> in $t$, is never zero, and starts at one, so it is one throughout: $e^{tK}\in SO(3)$. Conversely, differentiating $(e^{tK})^Te^{tK}=I$ at zero gives $K^T+K=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{e^{tK}\in SO(3)\text{ for all real }t\iff K^T=-K.}
$$
The quantifier matters: membership at one isolated time alone would not imply skewness.

Write $P(F)=DW(F)$ and $J=\det F>0$. For any skew matrix $K$, differentiate frame indifference along $e^{tK}F$:
$$
0=P(F):(KF)=(P(F)F^T):K.
$$
A matrix orthogonal to every skew matrix is symmetric. Therefore $P(F)F^T$ and the <Cauchy stress tensor> $T=J^{-1}P(F)F^T$ are symmetric.

Let $\mathcal C=D^2W(I)$ and assume $P(I)=0$. Differentiating symmetry of $P(F)F^T$ at $I$ in direction $H$ shows $\mathcal C[H]$ is symmetric for every $H$. Hence $c_{ijkl}=c_{jikl}$. As a <Hessian>, $\mathcal C$ also has pair-interchange symmetry $c_{ijkl}=c_{klij}$. Combining the two gives $c_{ijkl}=c_{ijlk}$. Equivalently $\mathcal C$ annihilates <skew-symmetric matrices>[infinitesimal rotations]. This proves both requested minor symmetries and shows exactly where the stress-free assumption enters. It is <stress-free linearization of an objective energy>.

For the particular energy, $\sum_iv_i^2=\operatorname{tr}(F^TF)$ and $\prod_iv_i=J$, so
$$
P(F)=4\alpha F+h'(J)\operatorname{cof}F.
$$
At $I$ this is $[4\alpha+h'(1)]I$. Therefore the reference is stress-free exactly when $\boxed{h'(1)=-4\alpha}$. To differentiate again, use $\operatorname{cof}F=JF^{-T}$. The <determinant> and inverse derivatives at $I$ give
$$
\boxed{D(\operatorname{cof})(I)[H]=(\operatorname{tr}H)I-H^T,}
$$
which also proves the supplied hint. It follows that
$$
\mathcal C[H]=4\alpha H+h''(1)(\operatorname{tr}H)I-4\alpha[(\operatorname{tr}H)I-H^T].
$$
For a symmetric $e$ this becomes
$$
\boxed{\mathcal Ce=8\alpha e+[h''(1)-4\alpha](\operatorname{tr}e)I=2\mu e+\lambda(\operatorname{tr}e)I,\quad\mu=4\alpha,\ \lambda=h''(1)-4\alpha.}
$$
No additional positivity of the <bulk modulus> follows from the stated smoothness of $h$ alone; this calculation identifies the moduli rather than imposing extra material assumptions.

= 2
{parent=2009}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The first <Piola-Kirchhoff stress tensor> $T_R$ maps a reference normal to force per reference area. The <Cauchy stress tensor> $T$ maps a current normal to force per current area. <Nanson's formula> gives $\mathbf n\,da=JF^{-T}\mathbf N\,dA$. Equating the actual surface force in both descriptions gives $T_R=JTF^{-T}$, and therefore
$$
\boxed{T=J^{-1}T_RF^T.}
$$
For <incompressibility> $\boxed{J=1}$. The strain-energy derivative only fixes the constitutive stress up to a <constraint reaction in hyperelastic stress>: $T_R=DW-p\operatorname{cof}F$, hence $T=DW F^T-pI$. The scalar $p$ is a <Lagrange multiplier> determined by equilibrium and boundary <tractions>, rather than by the isochoric energy alone.

In <principal stretch> axes, the extra <principal stress> is $v_i\partial W/\partial v_i=2\alpha v_i^2-2\beta v_i^{-2}$, up to that arbitrary spherical reaction. With $B=FF^T$, its <eigenvalues> are $v_i^2$, so isotropy yields
$$
\boxed{T^*=2\alpha B-2\beta B^{-1},\qquad T=T^*-pI.}
$$
For the prescribed <simple shear>,
$$
F=\begin{pmatrix}1&\gamma&0\\0&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\quad
B=\begin{pmatrix}1+\gamma^2&\gamma&0\\\gamma&1&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\quad
B^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1&-\gamma&0\\-\gamma&1+\gamma^2&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The $x_3$ faces retain normal $\pm\mathbf e_3$, and zero <traction> there gives $p=2(\alpha-\beta)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{T=2\gamma\begin{pmatrix}\alpha\gamma&\alpha+\beta&0\\\alpha+\beta&-\beta\gamma&0\\0&0&0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
This constant stress satisfies the interior equilibrium equation, and the remaining face forces maintain the deformation.

To compute the angle, the material face $x_1=1$ becomes the spatial plane $y_1-\gamma y_2=1$. Its outward <unit normal> and an in-plane unit tangent are
$$
\mathbf n=\frac{(1,-\gamma,0)}{\sqrt{1+\gamma^2}},\qquad\mathbf m=\frac{(\gamma,1,0)}{\sqrt{1+\gamma^2}}.
$$
Resolve the current <traction> $\mathbf t=T\mathbf n$ into these directions:
$$
N=\mathbf n\cdot T\mathbf n=-\frac{2\gamma^2(\alpha+2\beta+\beta\gamma^2)}{1+\gamma^2},\qquad
S=\mathbf m\cdot T\mathbf n=\frac{2\gamma(\alpha+\beta)}{1+\gamma^2}.
$$
Since $N<0$ for nonzero $\gamma$, the angle lies above $\pi/2$, and
$$
\boxed{\theta-\frac\pi2=\arctan\left(\frac{|\gamma|(\alpha+2\beta+\beta\gamma^2)}{\alpha+\beta}\right).}
$$
Using $\arctan z\sim z$ at zero gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{\gamma\to0}\frac{\theta-\pi/2}{|\gamma|}=\frac{\alpha+2\beta}{\alpha+\beta}.}
$$
Using the undeformed normal $\mathbf e_1$ would give the wrong coefficient. The transformed <unit normal> is essential to this <simple-shear traction angle for a Mooney-Rivlin solid>.

= 3
{parent=2009}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The <material symmetry group> consists of invertible admissible reference transformations $H$ for which $W(FH)=W(F)$ for every admissible $F$. Its rotational part lies in $SO(3)$; a <determinant>-one reference convention restricts the full group to the corresponding special linear group. This right action is distinct from the spatial left action $W(QF)=W(F)$ of <material frame indifference>.

The proper cube-rotation group has $\boxed{24}$ elements. Choose the image of one outward face normal in six ways, then the image of an adjacent perpendicular normal in four ways; orientation fixes the third direction. No reflections are included. For an isotropic material every proper rotation belongs to the <material symmetry group>.

Combine <material isotropy> with the physical frame-indifference condition. The <singular value decomposition> may be written $F=R\operatorname{diag}(v_1,v_2,v_3)Q$ with $R,Q\in SO(3)$ because $\det F>0$. Left and right rotational invariance remove $R,Q$. Signed permutation rotations permit any permutation of the three diagonal stretches, so
$$
\boxed{W(F)=\Phi(v_1,v_2,v_3),\qquad\Phi\text{ symmetric in its three arguments}.}
$$
Right invariance alone, without the usual <material frame indifference> assumption, would not suffice to remove spatial orientation.

The strict <Baker-Ericksen inequalities> say that larger distinct principal stretches carry larger principal Cauchy stresses:
$$
\boxed{(\sigma_i-\sigma_j)(v_i-v_j)>0\quad\text{if }v_i\ne v_j,\qquad
\sigma_i=J^{-1}v_i\Phi_i.}
$$
A <natural state of a hyperelastic material> has zero stress. If two stretches differed, their equal zero stresses would contradict the strict inequality. Thus all three equal some $v>0$, and <polar decomposition> gives $\boxed{F=vQ}$, $Q\in SO(3)$. A weak non-strict inequality alone would not prove this conclusion, so its strict convention is explicit here.

For the radial deformation put $f(R)=r(R)/R$, $R=|\mathbf x|$, $\boldsymbol\theta=\mathbf x/R$. Since $\partial_jR=\theta_j$,
$$
D\mathbf y=fI+Rf'\boldsymbol\theta\otimes\boldsymbol\theta
=\boxed{\frac rR I+\left(r'-\frac rR\right)\boldsymbol\theta\otimes\boldsymbol\theta.}
$$
This symmetric tensor has radial <eigenvalue> $r'$ and the repeated tangential <eigenvalue> $r/R$. Both are positive in the open annulus under the stated monotonicity/nonnegativity conditions. Therefore these are its principal stretches. The two cases below establish <radial zero-energy rigidity> by different arguments.

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

At every point the <deformation gradient> attains the differentiable energy's minimum, so $DW(D\mathbf y)=0$ and it is a natural state. By the strict Baker-Ericksen argument just proved, all its principal stretches are equal. In the radial formula this gives
$$
r'(R)=r(R)/R,\qquad\frac d{dR}\left(\frac{r(R)}R\right)=0.
$$
Thus $r(R)=vR$ for one constant $v>0$ on the <connected> radial interval, and
$$
\boxed{\mathbf y(\mathbf x)=v\mathbf x.}
$$
The constancy is global through the annulus, not a separately chosen dilation at each point.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The radial gradient $F=D\mathbf y$ is symmetric positive definite. If it belongs to a well $SO(3)U_i$, write $F=QU_i$. Then $F^TF=U_i^2$, while positive symmetry makes $F$ itself the unique <positive square root of an operator> of $F^TF$. Hence $F=U_i$. Likewise a <symmetric positive definite matrix> in $SO(3)$ must be $I$. Therefore
$$
D\mathbf y(\mathbf x)\in\{I,U_1,\ldots,U_N\}.
$$
For each radius, the tangential stretch $r(R)/R$ must belong to the finite set of eigenvalues of these matrices. The ratio is <continuous> on the <connected> radial interval, so it is one constant $v>0$. Thus $r(R)=vR$ and differentiation gives $r'=v$ as well. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbf y(\mathbf x)=v\mathbf x.}
$$
This proof uses finiteness and positive definiteness of the wells, not isotropy or the <Baker-Ericksen inequalities>. It also covers $R_1=0$: the punctured three-dimensional ball remains <connected>, and no condition at the excluded center was used.
